    'female|a||Across these five parts we build one thing: a panel of wage income for everyone born in 1985, from age twenty-five to thirty-seven — and then read the gender gap off it, year by year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The single headline number — Norwegian women earn about eighty-seven percent of men''s average monthly wage — hides the question that matters: at what age does that gap open?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||So we continue the course script: one dataset, sex and birth year-month imported for the whole population.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Two keeps fence off the cohort: born in 1985, so they turn twenty-five in 2010 and thirty-seven in 2022 — roughly fifty thousand people.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Those are exactly the years when careers steepen and first children arrive, which is why this window is the one worth following.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Now the wage itself: one import of INNTEKT_WLONN, carrying a date.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||That date is a lookup key into an annual accumulated variable rather than a measurement at a moment, so it selects an income year — verify which one in the metadata catalogue before you trust it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Want 2011 as well? That is a second import — and it cannot land in the same alias.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Every import needs its own name, so the year quietly migrates into the alias: wage2010, wage2011.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Thirteen years, thirteen import lines, thirteen aliases, ending at wage2022.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a|grave|And this is the line where the naive script stops being a panel.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Here is the table it actually builds: a person column, then one wage column per year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||These are synthetic persons — the real ones never leave the server — and each gets exactly one row.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Thirteen columns wide and one row per person, however many years you import.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Quick check. How many rows does this script give each person?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One row per person — the years arrived as columns, not as rows.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And the wide shape hides a distinction you will need: the zero in 2011 is a real value, someone with no wage income, while the blank in 2022 is a person who has left the register.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||What we want instead is fifty thousand people times thirteen years — about six hundred and fifty thousand person-year rows, with the year itself a variable.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a|grave|In the wide table the year exists only inside the column names, and no tabulate, summarize or regression can group by a name.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Nor can you fix it by looking: microdata.no, built by Statistics Norway with NSD, never shows you a single row, so the reshaping has to be reasoned about rather than eyeballed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Why can't this table answer "what did twenty-five-year-olds earn"?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Age is encoded in the column names, so there is no variable to group by.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||So you can now see the defect precisely: thirteen imports bought thirteen variables, not thirteen years. Next part, we make the year a column — and fifty thousand rows become six hundred and fifty thousand.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Now that we have seen what thirteen years in one table looks like, let''s build it: thirteen imports, explicit dates, and names that remember the year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Sex arrives with no date at all — the datastore holds BEFOLKNING KJOENN as a constant of the person, so one import gives one column and there is no year to choose.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Wage income is the opposite: it changes every year, so INNTEKT WLONN takes a date, and an alias we pick ourselves.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||That date is not a timestamp of when anything was measured; it selects the reference period, and for an annual income variable that is the calendar year the date falls in.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A quick check. Each wage import carries a date. What is the date doing?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||It selects the reference period — the value INNTEKT WLONN holds for the year that date falls in.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And the alias carries the year with it: wage2010, not wage.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||In a wide panel the column name is the only place the year is recorded, so reusing one alias across thirteen imports throws the time dimension away.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||So we write the years out: eleven through fourteen are the same line with two numbers changed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A loop over the years is legal here — for y in 2010 to 2022, no parentheses and no nesting — but thirteen visible lines let you see at a glance which years you actually asked for.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||2015 is worth stopping at.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||From the first of January 2015, Norwegian employers report wages monthly through a-ordningen — one shared report to the tax authority, NAV and Statistics Norway — so this panel crosses a change in how the numbers are collected, not only in what people earned.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The rest, out to 2022: each line adds exactly one column, and none of them touches the rows.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Then one printing command on the first year and the last, to confirm that both columns really arrived.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And there they are — wage income in nominal kroner, thirteen years apart.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Nominal: Norwegian consumer prices rose roughly forty percent between 2010 and 2022, and 5.8 percent in 2022 alone, so part of any rise across these columns is just the price level — deflating is your job, not the datastore''s.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Step back from the code and look at the table you are now holding.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Thirteen wage columns standing beside sex, one column per year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The names run from wage2010 to wage2022, so any later command can name a single year without you remembering an import order.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And the rows have not multiplied: thirteen measurements are stretched sideways across one person.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||What is the unit of analysis in this dataset?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One row per person — the years sit side by side as columns, which is exactly what wide means.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a|grave|That is the whole trick of importing a panel: explicit dates so every column is a known year, and year-suffixed aliases so the table documents itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Next we lay it on its side — thirteen columns becoming one wage column and one year column, so the row becomes a person-year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Last time we filled thirteen columns with wage income — and a panel in that shape cannot answer a single question about age.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Sex and birth month arrive as one row per person, because the person is the datastore's native unit.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And here is the loop from part two: one import per income year, each landing in a column of its own.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Look where the year lives — inside the variable name. It is metadata, not data.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||So: about fifty thousand people born in 1985, thirteen wage columns beside them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||You cannot condition on a year that has no column, and no regression accepts an age variable that does not exist — so today we turn those columns into rows.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||This is the shape we want: one row for every person in every year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Person one, income year 2010, age 25, one wage value — illustrative rows, since real ones never leave the platform.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The same person again and again, the age column climbing with the year, down to age 37 in 2022.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Then the next person starts underneath, and the rows simply stack.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Two ordinary columns now do the work the column names were doing — and nothing was added to the data; the year was always there, just unreadable.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Quick check before we write it. What does one row of this long table represent?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One person in one year — the unit of analysis moved from the person to the person-year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||There is no reshape command here, so you build the long table the way this language wants — one income year at a time, in its own dataset.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The cohort filter from lecture six, and then the money: not wage underscore 2010 any more, just wage.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The year that was hiding in the old column name is written out as a column of its own.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And age follows from it — year minus 1985 — since every person in this dataset shares one birth year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||collapse here takes a single grouping key, so sex and age are pasted into one composite string first — a workaround you will use constantly.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Then means by that key, and each pass of the loop contributes its rows.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Six hundred and fifty thousand person-years, and after lecture eight you know none of them leave individually — the long table you can actually read is the aggregated one.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||With age as a genuine column, the payoff is one plot instead of thirteen.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Mean wage income for the cohort's men, plotted straight against age — the shape, not yet the numbers; part five puts the real means here.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The women start close behind at 25 — this cohort's women are the better-educated half — and then the paths part.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Norwegian women's average monthly earnings have sat near 88 per cent of men's in recent years, and annual wage income, which counts hours as well as rates, diverges more.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Kleven, Landais and Søgaard's Danish register study puts most of that divergence around first births — a long-run earnings penalty of roughly twenty per cent that never closes.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: nb-NO-Chirp3-HD-Puck
    female|a||Which brings the fine print in your own age column back into view.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a|grave|One cohort means age and calendar year are the same column twice, so this widening is the life cycle and thirteen years of Norwegian wage growth welded together — inseparable until you import more cohorts.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Reshaping was never cosmetic: the long table is what lets age be a variable at all, and it is also what puts that confound on the page where you can see it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now that we have folded thirteen wide columns into one long panel, the panel is tidy and quietly wrong about absence — and if you report durations from it, you will overstate them.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Every import so far has been one of these: a vertical line through a single date, asking what was true that morning.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Underneath lies a life. A woman in our 1985 cohort is signed off sick on the second of March 2015 and returns on the twenty-ninth of May — sixty-three working days, one unbroken episode.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The following August she starts parental leave and stays on it until the end of June 2017, eleven months straddling a new year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And in September 2017, eighteen days off sick.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Each one is stored as a person, a start date, an end date and a value — an event variable, an interval rather than an instant.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Ask the register what she was doing on each first of January, and it answers: no, no, on leave, no.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a|grave|Two of her three episodes leave no trace — the register kept them; our question threw them away.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The language keeps the two shapes apart. This is the one we know — a dataset, and an import pinned to a single day.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||That date carries the whole question; move it two months and a different set of women is on leave.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The event import takes an interval instead, and the register returns everything that happened inside it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Three years in one call: the episodes are found and their lost day-equivalents summed per person, so the unit is exposure rather than status.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Summarize now describes days lost across 2015 to 2017 — a quantity you can add up.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||With one liability you have just accepted: any spell starting before your window or ending after it is clipped by your own two dates, so short follow-up manufactures short durations.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now back to the picture, because the bias in the snapshot panel is visible in it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Of three episodes, the snapshots caught exactly one — the eleven-month one.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Not luck: a fixed date lands inside a spell with probability proportional to that spell''s length.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Eighteen days never had a chance; eleven months could hardly be missed.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a|grave|So the snapshot panel does not simply see less — it draws a length-biased sample: too few episodes, and every survivor longer than typical.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A quick check. Compared with the spells themselves, what does a 1 January snapshot of sickness absence do?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A date falls inside a spell in proportion to its length, so short episodes vanish and the survivors look longer than typical.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Three things a yearly panel cannot give you at all: duration, timing relative to another event, and person-time at risk — the denominator under every rate you will report.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Timing is the one that reorganised this literature: Kleven, Landais and Søgaard lined Danish parents up by years since the first birth instead of calendar year, and mothers'' earnings drop around twenty percent below the counterfactual and stay there.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: nb-NO-Chirp3-HD-Puck
    female|a||A January wage column cannot tell you which January came after the birth, so event time is unreachable without event dates.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And these spells were built to pay people, not to describe them: the Norwegian employer covers the first sixteen days, so a benefit spell can open on day seventeen, and one illness can appear as two records when the payer or the rate changes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One more. In a benefit register, what most directly determines a spell's end date?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The end date is booked when the payment stops, so a closure can be administrative rather than medical — read the rules before calling it a duration.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||For our cohort, then: keep the yearly panel for wage, education and municipality, where a January reading is a fair description of a state — and go to event data for the leave, because the gap opens in the months between the lines.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a|grave|You can now see why: a snapshot reports what someone was on a date, a spell reports what happened and for how long, and only the second can be summed — or lined up against a birth.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now that the panel can hold spells as well as snapshots, we can draw the figure this series was built for — and the whole point of drawing it carefully is that a gap this striking invites a causal story the data cannot carry.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Three variables, the same three as in lecture one: sex, birth year and month, and wage income at one date.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Destring makes the year-month a number, and two keeps fence the cohort — everyone born in the twelve months of 1985, nobody else.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Collapse turns the roughly fifty thousand people born in Norway that year into two numbers: mean wage income for men, and for women.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Run it once and you get one point; run the panel version from part two — thirteen import dates, collapsed by sex and age — and you get thirteen, on each of two lines.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Ages twenty-five to twenty-eight, 2010 to 2013: both lines climb together from a bit over three hundred thousand kroner, women at ninety-five percent of men. Rounded means, so your run will differ in the decimals, not the shape.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Carry it to thirty-three and the lines come apart — the men's keeps its slope, the women's bends away.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||From thirty-four to thirty-seven the new distance simply persists: seven hundred and five against five hundred and forty-five thousand kroner.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Ninety-five percent at twenty-five, eighty-seven at thirty, seventy-seven at thirty-seven — and then flat.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The bend is the thing to see: women''s wage income does not fall, it stops accelerating while the other line carries on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Almost the whole divergence sits in a five-year window around thirty — and the mean age at first birth for Norwegian women is just about thirty.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Kleven, Landais and Søgaard found this same hinge in Danish registers and named it the child penalty: a long-run earnings gap near twenty percent that opens at the first birth and does not close.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: nb-NO-Chirp3-HD-Puck
